brier            ( outcomes probabilities -- f ) statistics.telic: Brier score — mean of (probability − outcome)² over n×1 vectors; NaN elements are skipped
column-means     ( mat -- mat' )          matrix.telic: 1×c of per-column means
cross-validate   ( units n-folds fit-xt score-xt -- fold-losses ) statistics.telic: k-fold cross-validation — units deal round-robin into folds in the order given (shuffle first when order matters; reuse one shuffle to compare configurations on the same folds); per fold, fit ( train-units -- model ) then score ( model test-units -- loss ), both taking everything from the stack since they run in this word's frame; answers the per-fold losses as an n-folds vector (mean it for the CV estimate, std for the standard error). A unit is whatever the array holds — rows, or per-cluster index arrays for cluster CV
mean             ( mat -- f )             matrix.telic: the arithmetic mean of the elements, NaNs skipped
ranks            ( v -- v' )              statistics.telic: 0-based midranks as nx1 — tied values share the mean of their sorted positions, NaNs rank last in index order
row-means        ( mat -- mat' )          matrix.telic: r×1 of per-row means
se               ( mat -- f )             statistics.telic: standard error of the mean (standard deviation ÷ √n)
summary          ( v/dataset -- fr )      statistics.telic: a vector answers { :min :q1 :median :mean :q3 :max } over its finite elements — a dimensioned vector in its unit, an instant vector (unit exactly s) with each statistic rendered as an ISO time string — plus :missing with the NaN count when any; an all-missing vector answers { :missing n }, an empty one { }. A dataset answers that frame per numeric column and { :distinct } (distinct non-missing cells, plus :missing) per text column, keyed by column name; any other column value errors naming the column
: 2dup over over ;
map ( arr/set xt -- arr ) or ( dataset xt -- dataset )
  Apply xt to each element; xt must leave exactly one value. datasets.telic extends it to a dataset: xt maps each row frame to a new row frame — derive, rename, or drop fields — and the returned frames rebuild into a dataset, so all rows must share keys and columns re-infer their representation
  ops 2 + n·xt, alloc 1a(n); dataset rows + new columns, O(n·xt); dataset O(n·(xt + k log k))

  > [ 1 2 3 ] [: dup * :] map . cr
  [ 1 4 9 ]
telic — a quick start:
  3 4 + .            add two numbers and print the result
  .s                 show the whole stack (clear empties it)
  [ 1 2 3 ]          an array
  { :a 1 }           a frame (symbol-keyed)
  [< 1 2 3 >]          a set
  : sq dup * ;       define a word
  5 sq .             run it (prints 25)
  words              list every word
  "mean" apropos     find words by name or summary
  ' 2dup see         print a word's definition
  help map           print one word's reference entry
  bye                quit
 frobenius-norm   ( mat -- f )             Euclidean (L2) norm: √(Σ aᵢⱼ²) over all elements — a vector's length; for a matrix the Frobenius (entrywise 2-)norm, not the spectral norm
norm             ( mat -- f )             matrix.telic: Euclidean (L2) norm, √(Σ elements²) — a vector's length, a matrix's Frobenius norm
